  "query_preview": "(Today through Monday)...\nIssued at 231 AM EDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nA vort max is currently over the eastern Great Lakes pushing \neastward ahead of a trough. An associated cold front will follow \nallowing for some lift and convection over Northern Indiana and \nOhio. This forcing will near NE portions of central Indiana this \nmorning, leading to some potential for scattered convection. \nParameter spacing is a bit more lack-luster than yesterday with \naround 1000 J/kg CAPE and 20-30kt of effective shear. This should be \nenough for a few persistent clusters, but overall any storms should \nremain rather weak with gusts below 35 MPH.  \n\nThe overall synoptic pattern will remain mostly similar for the \nmiddle of the week with one caveat. Cyclonic vorticity advection on \nthe northward side of the upper jet will aid pressure depletion over \nthe Mid Atlantic, digging the trough over Eastern Canada southward. \nThis, in combination with a weak frontal passage will shift \ntemperatures back towards seasonal across the area, and induce \nsubsidence over the region.  The resulting weather will be rather \nmundane, with highs in the low to mid 80s and dew points in the low \nto mid 60s. \n\nAs we near the end of the week a broad upper-level ridge centered \nover the northern plains will begin to propagate towards the Ohio \nValley promoting upper-level divergence. Dewpoints should climb once \nagain into the upper 60s as the mid-level flow from the Gulf \ncontinues to transport moist air into the region. Later in the day, \na cold front will move through Indiana. This front will be weak, but \ncould provide enough forcing for convective thunderstorms. \n\nBy Saturday, the upper-level trough will have strengthened into an \narea of low pressure over the Ohio Valley Region. The increase of \ncyclonic vorticity in the region along with continued moisture \nadvection from the Gulf may lead to additional rain showers over the \ncourse of the day. Guidance supports the existence of weak \ninstability in the region with MUCAPE values greater than 500 J/kg, \nand thus thunder will also be possible for Saturday and Sunday.\n\nLong-term guidance varies a bit beyond Sunday, with two main \nsolutions regarding upper-level ridge and trough dynamics. The first \nscenario propagates a ridge northwestward from Georgia into the Ohio \nValley, effectively pinching off the low over Arkansas. The second \nscenario tracks the low eastward, allowing a broad ridge to build \nover the Midwest. Expected temperatures will likely differ some \ndepending on the outcome, but in either case, conditions favor a \ndrier start to the workweek",
